<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-network-science">
<title>Network Science</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Graph theory, linear algebra, probability, differential equations,
dynamical systems, optimization, statistics, and mathematical modeling.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>explain the purpose of network science;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish graphs from physical networks;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define vertices, edges, directed edges, and weighted edges;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>construct adjacency matrices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define degree, in-degree, and out-degree;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand degree distributions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize sparse and dense networks;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define walks, trails, paths, and cycles;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand connected components;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish weak and strong connectivity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define graph distance;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand diameter and average path length;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand clustering coefficients;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize triangles and transitivity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand local and global network structure;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define centrality;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute degree centrality;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand closeness centrality;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand betweenness centrality;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand eigenvector centrality;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize PageRank conceptually;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand Katz centrality conceptually;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand hubs and authorities conceptually;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define the graph Laplacian;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand Laplacian quadratic forms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize spectral graph theory;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand algebraic connectivity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize Fiedler vectors conceptually;</p>
</li>
<li>
